# The STI-PA root this application ships, for the iconectiv STAGING host
# (authenticate-api-stg.iconectiv.com) and for no other.
#
# The list-signing certificate of every CA list `stir/trust` accepts must
# chain to a pinned STI-PA root, and so must the CRL signer that comes
# down with the CRL. Nothing is trusted on first use: without a pin that
# anchors the signer, a refresh is refused.
#
# A shipped root pins only the host it belongs to. `stir/trust` maps
# hosts to files (`shipped`), and this file is the entry for the staging
# host: a refresh whose `stipa_api_url` names any other host -- the
# production default, https://authenticate-api.iconectiv.com, included --
# gets nothing from here, and a CRL is held to the shipped roots of the
# host it was fetched from. Trusted everywhere, this root let a
# production cluster pointed at staging, or anyone able to answer for
# production with a staging-signed list, install the staging list and
# its test STI-CAs as call anchors. Do not add another host's root to
# this file: give it a file of its own and an entry in the table.
#
# What is here:
#
#   STI-PA Root Certificate Test CA (iconectiv staging)
#     SHA-256 A2:F1:DD:B9:80:63:CD:91:18:A5:FC:F1:8C:E6:B2:AF:
#             2D:3D:9F:D1:27:B4:E5:42:54:5D:91:4B:18:15:92:3F
#     2023-08-24 to 2033-08-24, self-signed
#     It issues `CN=STI-PA CA List` and `CN=STI-PA CRL` on
#     authenticate-api-stg.iconectiv.com.
#
# What is NOT shipped, and how to add it: the production root. The
# production STI-PA (authenticate-api.iconectiv.com) answered nothing on
# the network this file was built from -- the TLS connection is closed
# without a reply, from `GET /api/v1/ca-list` and from the CRL alike --
# so the production root could not be fetched and verified, and a root
# nobody verified is worse than none.
#
# Before the first refresh against production, an operator uploads it
# with the role `stipa`:
#
#     POST /v2/stir/trust  {"kind":"ca","role":"stipa","pem":"<the production root>"}
#     sup stir_maintenance trust_upload stipa_root /path/to/root.pem
#
# which is `trust.upload_root(kz, trust.StipaRoot, pem, by)`. It is kept
# in the `stipa_roots` field of `trust-ca`, pins the STI-PA for every
# host, never anchors a call, and a refresh never overwrites it. A
# `"kind":"ca"` upload without a role (or `trust_upload ca`) is a call
# root instead, and pins nothing; so is a `roots` field left from before
# the roles. Get the root from iconectiv directly and check its
# fingerprint against what they publish; do not take it from the CA
# list it is meant to authenticate.
